WEBVTT

00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:02.160
CARLOS CAMACHO: Hello, everyone.

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:05.670
We're Carlos and Sharon from
Red Hat, and today we're going

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:09.660
to show you how to deploy an
MCP server from the MCP catalog

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:14.970
and then use it in a multi-agent
application that will diagnose

00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:19.380
and resolve support cases across
different partner ecosystems.

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:22.260
It will be only five
minutes, all live.

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:24.750
Let's go.

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:27.000
What are we going to
address in this demo?

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.700
We're going to facilitate
a support resolution

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:33.600
across different partners
within the same ecosystem.

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:38.040
We created a simple yet powerful
open-source multi-agentic system

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:40.380
that is designed to
allow agentic workflows

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:42.900
to diagnose and
resolve problems.

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:45.480
We're leveraging the
Azure MCP server

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:50.640
and Red Hat AI secure MCP
catalog within this solution.

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:52.650
We have different layers.

00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:54.750
The first one is the
user access layer,

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:58.080
which is a PatternFly web
application that allow us

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:00.990
to interact with
a chat interface

00:01:00.990 --> 00:01:04.770
with the different routing
and support agents.

00:01:04.770 --> 00:01:06.990
The first part is
the orchestration

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:10.200
and security layer, which
will be handling all the

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:14.250
authentication,
authorization, and audit.

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:17.250
Then, we have the routing
agent, which is in charge

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:22.710
of routing the requests from
the users to the specific agent

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:26.850
that is helping you on
troubleshooting your issues.

00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:31.590
In this particular case, we have
A2A as the communication layer,

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:36.490
and we use ADK to develop all
the agents we have in this demo.

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:41.130
So let's start with
the MCP catalog.

00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:44.760
The OpenShift AI MCP catalog
gives you pre-built MCP servers

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:48.120
that you can browse, pick,
and deploy to your cluster.

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:51.180
In this case, we will be
deploying the Azure MCP server

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:53.280
to our Red Hat
OpenShift cluster.

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:56.220
You just have to pick the
deployment name, the project,

00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:58.830
and set up all the
configurations details

00:01:58.830 --> 00:02:01.260
to connect to your Azure tenant.

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:04.260
The catalog will handle
the containerd image,

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:06.480
the configuration, and
all the authentication

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:08.730
through managed identity.

00:02:08.730 --> 00:02:11.520
In this case, we will not
be writing any glue code.

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:14.580
We won't be building
any custom integration.

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:17.670
We will just deploy from
the catalog the MCP server,

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:20.790
and we will consume that
as a regular workload.

00:02:20.790 --> 00:02:23.520
Once you configure all the
details, you just have to click

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:27.090
on "Deploy the Azure MCP
server," and you just need

00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:31.350
to wait until the operator runs
the deployment, and you have it

00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:34.030
up and running for you to use.

00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:39.840
Now, we will log in
into our quickstart.

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:41.670
I will use a user
with enough access

00:02:41.670 --> 00:02:45.480
to our Azure support agent
to show how it works.

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:48.240
In this case, you can see
that I have access to three

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:51.600
out of four agents, including
the Azure support agent,

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:55.080
and we are going to start asking
questions out of the box.

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:56.610
In this case, I'm asking

00:02:56.610 --> 00:03:00.480
about the Well-Architected
Framework for AKS.

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:04.980
The routing agent is detecting
the intent of our request,

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:07.350
and it's delegating
the execution

00:03:07.350 --> 00:03:08.850
to the Azure support agent,

00:03:08.850 --> 00:03:12.510
which is wrapping the
Azure MCP server,

00:03:12.510 --> 00:03:14.160
and we can see the output

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:16.860
and all the MCP tools
that were executed.

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:20.910
Usually, the first one is about
the intent of our request,

00:03:20.910 --> 00:03:25.140
and the second one is the actual
execution of the MCP tool,

00:03:25.140 --> 00:03:27.960
giving the correct answer.

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:30.690
We can ask for other
things, like, for example,

00:03:30.690 --> 00:03:35.280
best practices for developing
Azure web applications.

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:39.720
In the same way, the routing
agent is detecting the intent.

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:42.360
It's doing the routing
of the request.

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:47.370
And then we have the specific
MCP tools being executed.

00:03:47.370 --> 00:03:51.060
In this case, we can see that
the output is a markdown file

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:55.800
with all these best practices
we were asking about.

00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:59.370
We can see that the routing
agent delegated the execution

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:02.400
to the Azure support
agent, we see the output,

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:06.180
and then at the end, we see the
MCP tools that were called.

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:09.630
Again, the first one about
the intent, and second one

00:04:09.630 --> 00:04:13.170
with the actual output we're
seeing in the chat window.

00:04:13.170 --> 00:04:16.560
We can also see the audit
log of the applications

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:21.960
because we store all the
events using OpenTelemetry.

00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:25.980
And we have the Audit Trail with
all the security events stored,

00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:29.670
and we can see, for example,
all the users have access

00:04:29.670 --> 00:04:34.040
to specific groups,
and we can see

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:38.430
if the requests were
accepted or denied.

00:04:38.430 --> 00:04:43.080
Now, we will test again the
quickstart, but using a user

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:45.990
without access to
any of the agents.

00:04:45.990 --> 00:04:48.150
I will log in as Josh.

00:04:48.150 --> 00:04:51.780
You can see here that Josh
doesn't have access to any

00:04:51.780 --> 00:04:55.250
of the agents, and when
we ask a question,

00:04:55.250 --> 00:04:59.450
the routing agent will
deny this request.

00:04:59.450 --> 00:05:04.100
In the same way, we can see
this event in the audit log,

00:05:04.100 --> 00:05:08.670
or we can see the traces
in the audit trail.

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:13.530
In this case, you can see
that Josh doesn't belong

00:05:13.530 --> 00:05:16.920
to any department, so any
of the agents will be able

00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:20.100
to answer his requests.

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:22.040
And this is all.

00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:23.580
Thank you.
